le class=\"tbl\">{39}</table>\n    {40}": "<h3>{0}Public finance</h3>\n    <p class=\"note\">Values are set finely with the sliders (the range spans the 1st–99th percentile of values used by real countries; the value is set on release). Changing the tax rate, policy rate or deficit cap costs {1} political capital. Allocating the budget costs none. Every change takes effect in each system once the news reaches it at light speed{2}. The size of the effects is provisional.</p>\n    <table class=\"tbl\">\n      <tr><td>Tax rate</td><td>{3}</td><td colspan=\"2\" class=\"muted wrap\">Tax on income (×{5} the standard {4}%){6}. Subsistence income is not taxed</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Consumption tax</td><td>{7}</td><td colspan=\"2\" class=\"muted wrap\">Levied on household purchases (goods and services) and paid to the system government. Weighs most on low-income systems (regressive). Its effect on loyalty and the council depends on the combined burden with the tax rate</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Tariffs</td><td>{8}</td><td colspan=\"2\" class=\"muted wrap\">Levied on imports from other nations (paid by the buying firm to the buying system’s government). The higher the tariff, the more buyers turn to domestic sellers. Not applied to government purchases of materials or to aid from an overlord</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Central bank</td><td><select data-act=\"fiscal\" data-k=\"cb\" aria-label=\"How the policy rate is set\"><option value=\"1\"{9}>Delegate (Taylor rule)</option><option value=\"0\"{10}>Set it myself</option></select></td><td colspan=\"2\" class=\"muted wrap\">When delegated, the central bank sets the policy rate from inflation and the pace of the economy (neutral rate {11}%/yr + inflation + {12} × (inflation − target {13}%) + {14} × output gap). The neutral rate is higher when output per person grows faster. Moving the policy rate switches to “Set it myself”</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Policy rate</td><td>{15}</td><td colspan=\"2\" class=\"muted wrap\">The nominal rate. Borrowing and saving by firms and households, and the years goods spend in transit, respond to the real rate, net of inflation (now {16}%/yr) (over the transit time, import prices rise by a factor of e^(real rate × years)). Held below inflation, it makes inflation keep rising</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Prices</td><td class=\"num\">{17}%/yr</td><td colspan=\"2\" class=\"muted wrap\">Inflation (target {18}%). Price index {19} (start 1). The ledgers count in starting prices, so unexpected inflation reduces the real weight of savings and bonds by that much</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Long-term bond yield</td><td class=\"num\">{20}%/yr</td><td colspan=\"2\" class=\"muted wrap\">10-year bonds: expected policy rate + term premium + credit premium (bonds ÷ GDP × {21}%; now {22}%). If bonds ÷ GDP exceeds {23}%, the state defaults and does not repay {24}% of its bonds (savings shrink by that much)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Deficit cap</td><td>{25}</td><td colspan=\"2\" class=\"muted wrap\">Shortfalls are covered by borrowing in bonds, up to this amount per year (GDP of {26}: {27} cr/yr). Bonds {28} cr; can borrow now {29} cr. When cash on hand exceeds {30} months of tax revenue, the excess repays bonds. Bonds ÷ GDP up to {31}%</td></tr>\n      {32}\n      {33}\n    </table>\n    {34}\n    <h3>Budget (uses {37}% of {35}’s tax revenue of {36} cr/yr; up to {38}% in total)</h3>\n    <table class=\"tbl\">{39}</table>\n    {40}",
